----+----+----+----+\n     a    b    c    d    e    f    g    h \nYour valid moves are (src-dst-promotion, or src-dst):\n[Q: d1-f3, K: e1-e2, P: g2-f3]\nYour answer format should be: Answer: piece-name: move. \nPiece-name and move (src-dst-promotion or src-dst) should be directly copied EXACTLY from valid move list.\nExample of valid output format: N: a1-b3; P: e7-e8-q; Piece B: g5-f4.\nPlease select a move from your valid moves that maximize your probability of winning.\n', 'reasoning': '', 'output': "Evaluation:  \n- **Q: d1-f3**: Moving the queen to f3 develops it actively, supporting potential central control and eyeing the kingside. It also indirectly defends the g2 pawn if needed. However, it may be slightly premature to bring the queen out early without more development.  \n- **K: e1-e2**: This king move is unusual in the opening. While it connects the rooks slightly and gets the king off the back rank, it wastes time and does little for development or center control. It's generally suboptimal in open positions unless there’s a concrete tactical reason.  \n- **P: g2-f3**: This pawn move appears to support a knight on e4 or prepare for kingside expansion. However, it blocks the natural development square for the bishop on g2 (in a King’s Indian setup) and weakens the dark squares around the king, especially h3 and e3. Also, it doesn’t contribute to central tension or immediate threats.\n\nAmong these, **Q: d1-f3** stands out as the most principled move. It develops a piece with tempo potential, supports central play, and can coordinate with knights or pawns pushing in the center. It also eyes the weak f7-square, which is only defended by the black king.\n\nReason:  \nQ: d1-f3 is the best developing move among the options, increasing pressure on the center and preparing for harmonious piece play. The other moves either waste time (K: e1-e2) or create structural weaknesses without sufficient compensation (P: g2-f3).\n\nAnswer: Q: d1-f3"}
