----+----+----+----+\n     a    b    c    d    e    f    g    h \nYour valid moves are (src-dst-promotion, or src-dst):\n[B: c1-d2, K: e1-d1, K: e1-e2, K: e1-f2]\nYour answer format should be: Answer: piece-name: move. \nPiece-name and move (src-dst-promotion or src-dst) should be directly copied EXACTLY from valid move list.\nExample of valid output format: N: a1-b3; P: e7-e8-q; Piece B: g5-f4.\nPlease select a move from your valid moves that maximize your probability of winning.\n', 'reasoning': '', 'output': 'Evaluation:  \nThe current position shows that White has a relatively safe king on e1 (KWF0), with both rooks and bishops still in starting positions, except for the bishop on c1 which has moved (BWF0). Black has a pawn on d5 (PBF1) that just advanced two squares last move (en passant possible in theory, but not relevant here since no white pawn on c5 or e5). The black bishop on c3 (BBF1) is deep in White’s territory, possibly threatening central control.\n\nWhite’s valid moves are limited:\n- **c1-d2**: Moving the white-squared bishop to develop and support the center, blocking potential threats along the b1-h6 diagonal.\n- **e1-d1**: King retreating to d1 – unusual and passive, loses castling rights without clear benefit.\n- **e1-e2**: King moving to e2 – also very passive, blocks the f1-bishop and doesn’t improve safety.\n- **e1-f2**: King moving toward the corner, again highly passive and non-developing.\n\nAmong these, only **c1-d2** contributes to development and central control. It activates a piece, supports potential e4 or d4 pushes, and keeps options open. The king moves are all weakening and unnecessary at this stage, especially since the king is already relatively safe and castling rights would be lost upon moving it.\n\nReason:  \nDeveloping the bishop with **c1-d2** is the most principled move, following opening principles (develop minor pieces, control center). The other options involve moving the king unnecessarily, which is bad strategy in the opening and loses flexibility, particularly castling.\n\nAnswer: B: c1-d2'}
