rectly available unless the bishop moves.  \n- The king move K: e1-e2 or K: e1-f2 could help connect rooks or support the center, but moving the king early without necessity is risky.  \n- The bishop on f1 (BWF0) has good diagonal prospects: B: f1-a6 targets a7, but likely too early; B: f1-c4 is very strong — it develops to a key central square, pressures f7 (a known weakness in many openings), and supports potential attack. This is a highly desirable developing move.  \n- Comparing all, **B: f1-c4** stands out as a powerful development move, placing the bishop on an active diagonal targeting f7, supporting e5 or d5 breaks, and preparing for potential queenside coordination.\n\nReason:  \nDeveloping the light-squared bishop to c4 (B: f1-c4) maximizes piece activity, exerts immediate pressure on the center and f7-pawn, and aligns with classical opening principles. It improves White’s position significantly, whereas pawn moves like a4/h4 are less urgent and d6 is committal without full preparation. Bc4 is flexible, threatening, and hard to counter immediately for Black.\n\nAnswer: B: f1-c4"}
