----+----+----+----+\n     a    b    c    d    e    f    g    h \nYour valid moves are (src-dst-promotion, or src-dst):\n[P: a2-a4, P: a2-a3, B: c4-b3, B: c4-d3, B: c4-e2, B: c4-f1, B: c4-b5, B: c4-a6, B: d2-c1, B: d2-c3, B: d2-b4, B: d2-a5, B: d2-e3, B: d2-f4, B: d2-g5, B: d2-h6, P: d5-d6, K: e1-d1, K: e1-e2, K: e1-f1, P: g2-g4, P: g2-g3, R: h1-g1, R: h1-f1, P: h2-h4, P: h2-h3]\nYour answer format should be: Answer: piece-name: move. \nPiece-name and move (src-dst-promotion or src-dst) should be directly copied EXACTLY from valid move list.\nExample of valid output format: N: a1-b3; P: e7-e8-q; Piece B: g5-f4.\nPlease select a move from your valid moves that maximize your probability of winning.\n', 'reasoning': '', 'output': "Evaluation:  \nSeveral moves are worth considering:\n\n- **P: a2-a4 / a2-a3**: Pushing the a-pawn could aim to control b5 or prepare queenside expansion, but it's slow and doesn't pressure Black immediately. White already has a pawn on d5, so central focus may be better.\n\n- **B: c4-b3**: Targets the weak f7 square indirectly and pressures Black’s kingside. Also defends the d5 pawn if needed. A solid developing move with attacking potential.\n\n- **B: c4-e2**: Retreats slightly; less appealing as it removes the bishop from an active diagonal.\n\n- **B: c4-f1**: Moves to a passive square, blocking potential rook lift or king safety; not recommended.\n\n- **B: c4-b5+?**: Could be strong if it checks, but Black can block with ...c6 or ...Nc6 (though no knight is present). However, currently Black has no immediate cover — but b5 is defended by Black’s bishop on c4? No — wait, Black's dark-squared bishop is on d4? Wait — correction: in board, **BBF1** is on **d4**, so yes, **b5 is attacked by White’s bishop on c4**, and **b7 is a vulnerable point**.\n\nWait — rechecking board:\n\n- White bishop on **c4** (BWF1), Black bishop on **d4**? No — wait:  \n  - At **d4**: `BBF1` → Black Bishop, has moved, en passant irrelevant.  \n  So both bishops are on **c4 (White)** and **d4 (Black)** — they are on adjacent diagonals.\n\nThus, **B: c4-b5** attacks b5, which threatens to win material if Black has no defense — but what does it hit?\n\nAt **b7** is a Black pawn (PBF0), protected only by the king potentially. But **b5 is currently unoccupied**, so moving to b5 is safe.\n\nBut more importantly: **B: c4-b5 pins the b7 pawn against the king if there's no interposition** — and indeed, the b7 pawn is only defended by the king! So **B: c4-b5** applies pressure, and if Black doesn’t respond, White can follow up with Bxb7, winning a pawn with a significant advantage.\n\nAlternatively, **B: c4-b5** also indirectly supports a later d5-d6 push.\n\nAlso, **B: d2-c3 / d2-b4 / d2-a5** — the light-squared bishop on d2 is actually on a bad diagonal. But **d2-b4** eyes the kingside and could support an attack.\n\nBut best candidate seems **B: c4-b5** — active, aggressive, targets weakness near Black’s king.\n\nAnother idea: **d5-d6** — advancing the passed pawn. This could be strong: **d6** would make it hard for Black to stop promotion unless they use the king or pieces. But after **d5-d6**, Black plays ...Rf8 or ...Qe7? But Black has no queen yet developed. Still, **...Rf8** or even **...Kf7** might challenge it. But White can support with e.g. Be3 or Bf4 later.\n\nHowever, **d5-d6** loses the pawn if not supported: **...exd6??** but no ex, since no e-pawn. After **d5-d6**, Black can play **...Rf8**, but cannot capture because no piece on e-file. Actually, **d6 is supported only by the bishop on c4**, so if Black plays **...Rxd6??**, then **BxR**, winning rook. But Black won’t do that. Instead, they may just ignore it and counterattack.\n\nBut **d5-d6** creates a strong threat of queening. It’s a very forcing and dangerous move.\n\nCompare:\n- **B: c4-b5**: +1.5 advantage, pressures b7\n- **P: d5-d6**: +2.0 — creates a dangerous passer, possibly decisive\n\nAfter **d5-d6**, Black must deal with the threat of d7 and queening. Their pieces are undeveloped. The rook on f8 is misplaced, king is exposed.\n\nBlack’s best reply might be **...Rf8**, but that doesn’t stop d7. Or **...Be7**, but still, d7 comes next.\n\nBut wait — after **d5-d6**, Black plays **...Rf8**, then **d7**, then **Rf7**, stopping promotion. So timing matters.\n\nBut **d5-d6** is still strong — it forces Black to spend time stopping it.\n\nHowever, **what about checkmating chances?**\n\nWhite has bishops active, king somewhat safe, rook on h1 can become active.\n\nBut another idea: **R: h1-g1** or **h1-f1** — connecting rooks or preparing g-file attack? But not urgent.\n\nBest move is likely **B: c4-b5**, gaining material or forcing weaknesses, or **d5-d6** for initiative.\n\nBut let’s assess **tactical safety**:\n\nIs **d5-d6** safe?  \n- Currently, d5 is guarded by bishop on c4.\n- After d5-d6, the pawn is on d6, still guarded by nothing directly? Wait — bishop on c4 guards d5, but **not d6** — because c4 to d6 is not diagonal (c4-d5 is diagonal, but d6 is one further — c4 to d6 is one square over and two up — not diagonal).\n\nWait — **c4 to d5 is diagonal (c4, d5)** — yes, same color. But **d6 is one rank higher** — so c4 to d6 is c4→d5→e6, not aligned. Actually, **c4 and d6 are on the same diagonal** — c4, d5, e6 — so **d6 is on the same diagonal as c4**? Let's see:\n\n- c4 = file c, rank 4  \n- d5 = file d, rank 5 → +1,+1  \n- e6 = +2,+2  \n- So d6 = file d, rank 6 → difference from c4: +1 file, +2 ranks → not diagonal. Diagonal requires equal delta.\n\nSo **bishop on c4 does NOT guard d6**.\n\nTherefore, after **d5-d6**, the pawn is **undefended**!\n\nBlack can simply play **...Rxd6**, capturing the pawn, and White has no recapture.\n\nFor example:  \n1. d5-d6? Rf8-d8  \n2. ...Rxd6! And White cannot recapture — bishop on c4 doesn’t see d6.\n\nSo **d5-d6 loses the pawn immediately** — blunder.\n\nHence, **d5-d6 is illegal? No, it's legal, but losing**.\n\nSo **not playable**.\n\nTherefore, **must not play d5-d6**.\n\nNow, **B: c4-b5** — is it safe?\n\nFrom c4 to b5:  \n- b5 is empty — yes  \n- Is it attacked? By whom?  \n  - Black has bishop on d4 — does it attack b5?  \n    - d4 to b5: d4→c5→b6 — no, b5 is d4 + (-2 files, +1 rank) — not diagonal.  \n    - Delta: -2, +1 — not equal, so not diagonal.  \n  - Black has pawns: a7, b7, etc. — no pawn attacks b5.  \n  - Knight? None nearby.  \n  - King? No.\n\nSo **b5 is safe**.\n\nAfter **B: c4-b5**, the bishop attacks b7 pawn, which is defended only by the king. Black will likely have to respond with ...a6 or ...Bb6 or ...Nd7, but all waste time.\n\nThis gains tempo and threatens material gain.\n\nOther moves like **g2-g4** or **h2-h4** are flank pushes without clear purpose.\n\n**K: e1-e2** — gets king out of back rank, but not urgent.\n\n**R: h1-f1** — prepares centralization, maybe supports e-file or f-file attack, but limited.\n\n**B: d2-b4** — pins something? Not really. Could be useful later.\n\nBut **B: c4-b5** stands out as most aggressive and effective.\n\nReason:  \nB: c4-b5 increases pressure on Black’s position, targets the weak b7 pawn (only defended by the king), and may win material or force positional concessions. It’s safe, active, and fits White’s development plan.\n\nAnswer: B: c4-b5"}
